# config/axes/Kconfig.security — The SECURITY axis and the mitigation switches it sets
#
# Owner decision (wave 11): every mitigation is a switch in `make config`;
# none is forced on. The threat model is the SECURITY posture
# (the choice below), which sets the DEFAULTS of the mitigation
# switches with `imply`, never `select`: each switch can still be turned off
# by hand under any posture. Only mitigations that exist in code
# have a symbol; the ones that do not are listed as comments.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECURITY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

choice
    prompt "Security posture (SECURITY)"
    default SECURITY_SEPARATION_STATIC if PRESET_INDUSTRIAL || PRESET_AUTOMOTIVE
    default SECURITY_HARDENED if PRESET_IOT_HMI || PRESET_GATEWAY || PRESET_DESKTOP || \
                                 PRESET_SERVER || PRESET_FIRMWARE || PRESET_WEARABLE
    default SECURITY_GENERAL
    help
      How much the image trusts what runs on it, and so who it defends
      against (the threat model, which was a separate THREAT choice until
      2026-10-22). It sets the default of OTA_SIG_MANDATORY, of the
      mitigation switches it implies below ("Mitigations", "Security") and,
      for Hardened, of KASLR (require); it never forces one (`imply`, not
      `select`), and a switch set by hand wins. Each posture includes the
      ones above it. Every mitigation that exists in code is on by default
      in every posture (MITIGATION_SPECTRE_V1_INDEX, INFER_MODEL_SIG_REQUIRED),
      so only the physical-access postures change a mitigation default.

    config SECURITY_GENERAL
        bool "General"
        help
          The base threat model: an image on the allow-list is compromised
          and attacks the kernel and the other images through syscalls and
          shared hardware. No default beyond the switches' own.

    config SECURITY_HARDENED
        bool "Hardened"
        help
          Third-party code runs in ring 3 (applications, drivers, models; no
          mitigation specific to it exists in the tree yet, see the "not
          implemented" lines in config/axes/Kconfig.security). Unsigned OTA images are refused
          (OTA_SIG_MANDATORY starts on) and the kernel image must be slid at
          boot (KASLR starts at require: an image without a seed does not
          boot).

    config SECURITY_SEPARATION_STATIC
        bool "Separation, static"
        imply CONSOLE_LOCKDOWN
        imply SECURE_BOOT_ENFORCED
        imply OTA_SIG_MANDATORY
        imply LINK_PROVISIONING_ENFORCED
        help
          The device can be touched and its partitions are fixed at build
          time: the attacker can reach the console, the removable FAT
          volume (also exposed over USB mass storage) and the boot media.
          Unsigned OTA images are refused, and on top of the base posture it
          implies:
            CONSOLE_LOCKDOWN      no kernel console; the ring-3 user
                                  shell is unprivileged unless a signed
                                  topology row grants it more;
            SECURE_BOOT_ENFORCED  boot halts on an unsigned or badly
                                  signed kernel image ("Security");
            OTA_SIG_MANDATORY     unsigned OTA images are refused;
            LINK_PROVISIONING_ENFORCED
                                  boot halts without a link key, the
                                  handshake is refused on an unseeded
                                  entropy pool, and the UART bridge
                                  (brain-protocol frames outside any
                                  Noise session) is down.
          Each implied switch carries its own cost and deployment
          requirement; read its help before building with this posture.

    config SECURITY_CONFIDENTIAL
        bool "Confidential"
        imply CONSOLE_LOCKDOWN
        imply SECURE_BOOT_ENFORCED
        imply OTA_SIG_MANDATORY
        imply LINK_PROVISIONING_ENFORCED
        help
          As separation-static (the same implied switches), for workloads kept apart from the host:
          VIRT starts at `split`.

endchoice

menu "Mitigations"

config MITIGATION_SPECTRE_V1_INDEX
    bool "Spectre v1: mask table indices that come from ring 3"
    default y
    help
      Bounds-check-bypass mitigation, the analogue of Linux's
      array_index_nospec (crates/core/limits/src/nospec.rs). After the bounds
      check, the index used for the load is ANDed with a mask computed
      without a branch (riscv64: sltu+neg; aarch64: cmp+sbc+csdb), so a
      load issued under a mispredicted check reads entry 0, not memory
      past the table. Applied to the syscall table and to the per-task
      tables a syscall indexes with a user value (see the call sites of
      azos_limits::nospec).

      Cost, instructions per operation under -icount
      (tools/vsbench_aarch64.sh, this option y against n, same tree,
      2026-10-02), aarch64/riscv64:
        syscall-floor (getpid, answered before the table)    +0 / +0
        clock [call] (the syscall-table mask alone)          +5 / +4
        chan-call, notify, sensor-read-call (+ cap slot)  +9..10 / +7..8
        ipc-roundtrip                                         +9 / +12
        ring-pingpong                                        +36 / +19
        udp-roundtrip (socket fd masks on every call)        +62 / +140
      aarch64 adds one csdb per masked site (118 in a qemu kernel).

      x86_64 also fences the conditional swapgs of the trap entry (the
      swapgs variant, CVE-2019-1125): one lfence on every interrupt and
      exception entry (kernel/src/entry/x86_64/asm/trap_entry.S), none on
      a syscall.

      n: every masked site compiles to the unmasked index.

config INFER_MODEL_SIG_REQUIRED
    bool "Require an Ed25519 signature on the inference service's model data files"
    default y
    help
      The ring-3 inference service (userspace/services/infsrv) verifies the
      GGUF policy /fat/POLICY.GGF against /fat/POLICY.SIG before its start-up
      self-test parses it: a DSIG sidecar (magic, algorithm, length, the
      signature) over the file's bytes, the same key and format as CAPS.SIG and CONFIG.SIG
      (azos_topology::TRUSTED_PUBKEY; `make` signs it with
      tools/gen_config_sig.py). The file lives on the USB-exposed FAT volume.

      A policy file without a valid signature is refused with an
      "[GGUF] /fat/POLICY.GGF REFUSED" line and the self-test is skipped.

      The model bundles (*.MBL) the service serves through the Infer
      interface carry their own signature and are always verified: a bundle
      holds the cost table the admission believes, so there is no switch for
      them. The bundle of the behavior loop's MLP (BEHAVIOR.MBL) is one.

      Cost: one Ed25519 verification per file when the service starts,
      nothing per inference. The service image carries the verifier either
      way, for the bundles.

      n: the policy file is used unverified.

config CONSOLE_LOCKDOWN
    bool "Production console lockdown: no kernel console, unprivileged user shell"
    default n
    help
      Under this option the in-kernel recovery console (RECOVERY_CONSOLE) is
      not created at all, in safe mode included: boot prints
      "[CONSOLE] lockdown: no kernel console task". The console belongs
      to the ring-3 user shell (SH.ELF, USER_SHELL; with USER_SHELL=n
      the image has no console).

      The user shell's built-in row is reduced: it may start the
      unprivileged tool image (TOOLBOX.ELF) and write /tmp, nothing
      else. It holds no launch right on POWER.ELF (power suspend,
      reboot, shutdown, sched_hz) nor on FLIGHT.ELF, BEHAVIOR.ELF,
      CONFIG.ELF and OTA.ELF: they are refused at SYS_SPAWN_EX and
      recorded. It cannot change /fat. A privileged service shell
      exists only if the signed topology gives an image's row those
      capabilities; nothing on the console can grant them.

      The authority table (crates/core/ipc/src/authority_policy.rs) is
      unchanged by this option for ring 3: a typed syscall checks the
      calling image's own capability on every build. For the recovery
      console it names `reboot` and `shutdown` (WRITE on the `power` endpoint, checked
      on every build) and `config set watchdog_ms` and `ota rollback`
      (WRITE on the `config` and `ota` endpoints, only under this option,
      and the console would be seeded with no capability); the other
      commands of those families are ring-3 forms. Both matter only to a build that still has the
      recovery console, i.e. with this option off.

      Default n: the recovery console exists (it takes the console in
      safe mode, without SH.ELF, or when the user shell is given up
      on), it is seeded with Cap<Motor> and WRITE on the `power`, `config` and `ota` endpoints, and the
      two lockdown-only rows proceed unchecked. The check's predicate
      also answers "no" during a brain-loss containment episode, so
      enforcing those rows on every build would refuse `ota rollback`
      in the middle of one.

      Cost: none on any syscall path; a production image loses the
      in-kernel console as a recovery path.

config ELF_REFUSE_EXEC_HEADERS
    bool "ELF loader: refuse an executable segment that maps the ELF headers"
    default y
    help
      The loader maps every PT_LOAD with its own permissions (read-only,
      read-execute, read-write; W^X both ways), so `.rodata` in a segment
      of its own is never executable. A linker can still fold `.rodata`
      into the text segment: lld `--no-rosegment`, or GNU ld without
      `-z separate-code` (its default on riscv64 and aarch64), puts the ELF
      header, the program headers and every read-only section in one RX
      segment starting at file offset 0. The program headers are all the
      loader sees, and that is the signature they carry, so with this on
      an image whose executable segment covers the headers is refused at
      exec (`elf_bounds::check_exec_headers`): its read-only data would run
      as code. tools/elf_page_perms.py checks the built images, section
      headers included, at the page size the kernel maps.

      Every image the build makes, BusyBox (lld) included, keeps the
      headers out of its text segment. Off: such an image loads, its
      `.rodata` executable (what Linux does). Turn it off only to run an
      unmodified Linux binary linked that way; relinking it with
      `-z separate-code` or lld's default layout is the better fix.
      Cost: one comparison per PT_LOAD at exec; no RAM.

if ARCH_X86_64

config X86_UCODE_LOAD
    bool "Load CPU microcode at boot from a signed boot module"
    default y
    help
      Early microcode loading, as Linux does it: the boot CPU loads the
      newest patch for itself before the CPU features and the speculation
      mitigations below are probed (a patch adds MD_CLEAR, RFDS_CLEAR,
      GDS_CTRL, the Zenbleed and Inception fixes), and every secondary CPU
      loads the same patch as it comes up, before its own mitigation MSRs.

      The patch comes from a boot module, the signed microcode image
      `make ucode` builds (mk/firmware.mk) from the published Intel and AMD
      files: QEMU's `-initrd` on the PVH entry, or `module_path:` on the
      Limine ESP. The image is verified like any other signed image (an
      `RSIG` by the update key, SECURE_BOOT_PUBKEY) before a byte of it is
      parsed; then the Intel update (signature, platform flags, checksums,
      the extended signature table) or the AMD patch (the container's
      equivalence table) for this CPU is written to
      IA32_BIOS_UPDT_TRIG / MSR_AMD64_PATCH_LOADER and the revision is read
      back. Nothing is loaded under a hypervisor, which owns the microcode
      (Linux skips it too), and nothing older than the revision in force.
      Each outcome, with the revision before and after, is a `boot-chain`
      decision record (stage 6).

      Cost: none without a module. With one, a SHA-256 over the image and
      one signature check on the boot CPU (an image of every Intel and AMD
      file is about 18 MiB; one CPU's file is kilobytes), the matched patch
      kept in RAM for the secondaries, and one MSR write per CPU.
      n: no module is looked at; the CPU runs the firmware's microcode.

comment "x86_64 speculative-execution mitigations (n / probe / require)"
comment "Chosen by probe: ARCH_CAPABILITIES and CPUID first, the family/model table only for what has no bit."

choice
    prompt "Meltdown (rogue data cache load): kernel page-table isolation"
    default X86_MIT_MELTDOWN_PROBE
    help
      Intel cores that speculate a user load past the privilege check read
      kernel memory (CVE-2017-5754). Affected unless
      IA32_ARCH_CAPABILITIES.RDCL_NO says otherwise; AMD and Hygon cores never
      are. The mitigation is KPTI (a user page table without the kernel), which
      is NOT in the tree yet: on an affected CPU probe prints
      `meltdown=vulnerable` and require refuses to boot.
      n: not looked at. Cost when KPTI lands: a CR3 write per kernel entry and
      exit (PCID makes it a few tens of cycles).

    config X86_MIT_MELTDOWN_NEVER
        bool "n: do not look at it, do nothing"
    config X86_MIT_MELTDOWN_PROBE
        bool "probe: mitigate when the CPU is affected, say so when the tree cannot"
    config X86_MIT_MELTDOWN_REQUIRE
        bool "require: refuse to boot on an affected CPU the tree cannot mitigate"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "L1 terminal fault: PTE inversion"
    default X86_MIT_L1TF_PROBE
    help
      An Intel core without RDCL_NO that is not on the unaffected list reads
      L1D data through a not-present PTE's frame address (CVE-2018-3615/3620).
      PTE inversion is satisfied by construction: a not-present entry this
      kernel writes holds no frame (`mmu::empty`, `mmu::make_demand`), and
      frame 0, the one such an entry names, is never allocated (`boot_hooks`
      checks it after the PMM is up). Cost: none.
      n: not looked at.

    config X86_MIT_L1TF_NEVER
        bool "n: do not look at it, do nothing"
    config X86_MIT_L1TF_PROBE
        bool "probe: mitigate when the CPU is affected, say so when the tree cannot"
    config X86_MIT_L1TF_REQUIRE
        bool "require: refuse to boot on an affected CPU the tree cannot mitigate"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "MDS and TAA: clear the CPU buffers on return to ring 3"
    default X86_MIT_MDS_TAA_PROBE
    help
      Microarchitectural data sampling (CVE-2018-12126/12127/12130/11091) and
      TSX asynchronous abort (CVE-2019-11135) on Intel cores without
      MDS_NO / TAA_NO. The mitigation is `verw` on every return to ring 3
      (trap_entry.S, guarded by one byte), which the microcode makes clear the
      fill and store buffers (CPUID.(7,0):EDX[10] MD_CLEAR). Without MD_CLEAR
      the CPU is vulnerable. Cost: one `verw` (about 400 cycles) per return to
      ring 3 on an affected CPU, a compare of one byte elsewhere.
      n: not looked at, no `verw`.

    config X86_MIT_MDS_TAA_NEVER
        bool "n: do not look at it, do nothing"
    config X86_MIT_MDS_TAA_PROBE
        bool "probe: mitigate when the CPU is affected, say so when the tree cannot"
    config X86_MIT_MDS_TAA_REQUIRE
        bool "require: refuse to boot on an affected CPU the tree cannot mitigate"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "Register file data sampling"
    default X86_MIT_RFDS_PROBE
    help
      Intel Atom cores (Alder/Raptor Lake E-cores, Gracemont, Tremont,
      Goldmont) without RFDS_NO leak register-file data (CVE-2023-28746). The
      mitigation is the same `verw` as MDS, effective when the microcode sets
      ARCH_CAPABILITIES.RFDS_CLEAR. Cost: shared with MDS_TAA.
      n: not looked at.

    config X86_MIT_RFDS_NEVER
        bool "n: do not look at it, do nothing"
    config X86_MIT_RFDS_PROBE
        bool "probe: mitigate when the CPU is affected, say so when the tree cannot"
    config X86_MIT_RFDS_REQUIRE
        bool "require: refuse to boot on an affected CPU the tree cannot mitigate"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "Gather data sampling (Downfall)"
    default X86_MIT_GDS_PROBE
    help
      Intel Skylake through Rocket Lake cores without GDS_NO leak vector
      register data through gather (CVE-2022-40982). The mitigation is a
      microcode update; the tree keeps it enabled (IA32_MCU_OPT_CTRL bit 4
      clear) when ARCH_CAPABILITIES.GDS_CTRL says it can be controlled, and
      reports `vulnerable` when the microcode is missing or the firmware
      disabled and locked it. Cost: the microcode's own (gather is slower).
      n: not looked at, the mitigation is left as the firmware set it.

    config X86_MIT_GDS_NEVER
        bool "n: do not look at it, do nothing"
    config X86_MIT_GDS_PROBE
        bool "probe: mitigate when the CPU is affected, say so when the tree cannot"
    config X86_MIT_GDS_REQUIRE
        bool "require: refuse to boot on an affected CPU the tree cannot mitigate"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "Spectre v2 (branch target injection): eIBRS, AutoIBRS, else retpoline"
    default X86_MIT_SPECTRE_V2_PROBE
    help
      Indirect branches predicted from another context (CVE-2017-5715). In
      order of preference, chosen by probe:
        eIBRS      Intel, ARCH_CAPABILITIES.IBRS_ALL: IA32_SPEC_CTRL.IBRS set
                   once on every CPU;
        AutoIBRS   AMD, CPUID.80000021H:EAX[8]: EFER.AIBRS set on every CPU;
        retpoline  the kernel is built with the external indirect-branch thunk
                   (-Zretpoline-external-thunk) and the thunk
                   (crates/core/arch-x86_64/src/retpoline.rs) takes the
                   retpoline sequence.
      With hardware protection the boot patches the thunk once, on the
      boot CPU before the text is made read-only, to a plain `jmp *%r11`
      (a run-time test in front of the jump would let a mispredicted
      branch speculate it, which a retpoline exists to stop).
      probe: the thunk is built in; eIBRS/AutoIBRS when present, else retpoline.
        Cost: a compare and a direct branch per indirect call with eIBRS, the
        retpoline sequence (an unpredicted return, about 20-30 cycles) without.
      require: the kernel is built WITHOUT the thunk (no cost at all) and the
        boot refuses a CPU with neither eIBRS nor AutoIBRS.
      n: no thunk, nothing written: indirect branches are unprotected.

    config X86_MIT_SPECTRE_V2_NEVER
        bool "n: do not look at it, do nothing"
    config X86_MIT_SPECTRE_V2_PROBE
        bool "probe: mitigate when the CPU is affected, say so when the tree cannot"
    config X86_MIT_SPECTRE_V2_REQUIRE
        bool "require: refuse to boot on an affected CPU the tree cannot mitigate"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "Branch history injection"
    default X86_MIT_BHI_PROBE
    help
      Intel cores with eIBRS but no BHI_NO let history from another context
      steer an indirect branch (CVE-2022-0001/0002). The mitigation is
      IA32_SPEC_CTRL.BHI_DIS_S (CPUID.(7,2):EDX[4]); a CPU without eIBRS is
      covered by the retpoline. The software BHB-clearing loop is not in the
      tree: an eIBRS CPU with neither BHI_NO nor BHI_DIS_S is `vulnerable`.
      Depends on the Spectre v2 choice (n there leaves indirect branches open).
      Cost: the CPU's own, a few percent on indirect-branch-heavy code.
      n: not looked at.

    config X86_MIT_BHI_NEVER
        bool "n: do not look at it, do nothing"
    config X86_MIT_BHI_PROBE
        bool "probe: mitigate when the CPU is affected, say so when the tree cannot"
    config X86_MIT_BHI_REQUIRE
        bool "require: refuse to boot on an affected CPU the tree cannot mitigate"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "Retbleed (return instruction prediction)"
    default X86_MIT_RETBLEED_PROBE
    help
      Returns predicted from the indirect predictor (CVE-2022-29900/29901):
      Intel Skylake-era cores (and any core reporting ARCH_CAPABILITIES.RSBA),
      AMD Zen 1 and Zen 2 and Hygon family 18h without CPUID BTC_NO. An Intel
      core with eIBRS is covered by it. The Skylake-era mitigation (IBRS on
      every kernel entry) and AMD's untrained return thunk are NOT in the tree:
      such a CPU is `vulnerable`.
      n: not looked at.

    config X86_MIT_RETBLEED_NEVER
        bool "n: do not look at it, do nothing"
    config X86_MIT_RETBLEED_PROBE
        bool "probe: mitigate when the CPU is affected, say so when the tree cannot"
    config X86_MIT_RETBLEED_REQUIRE
        bool "require: refuse to boot on an affected CPU the tree cannot mitigate"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "Speculative return stack overflow (AMD)"
    default X86_MIT_SRSO_PROBE
    help
      AMD Zen 1 through Zen 5 mispredict a return from a poisoned return
      address (CVE-2023-20569). Not affected: CPUID SRSO_NO; or, with no guest
      hosted by this kernel, SRSO_USER_KERNEL_NO (Zen 4). The safe-RET and
      IBPB-on-entry mitigations are NOT in the tree: any other Zen CPU is
      `vulnerable`.
      n: not looked at.

    config X86_MIT_SRSO_NEVER
        bool "n: do not look at it, do nothing"
    config X86_MIT_SRSO_PROBE
        bool "probe: mitigate when the CPU is affected, say so when the tree cannot"
    config X86_MIT_SRSO_REQUIRE
        bool "require: refuse to boot on an affected CPU the tree cannot mitigate"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "Zenbleed (AMD Zen 2)"
    default X86_MIT_ZENBLEED_PROBE
    help
      Zen 2 parts (family 17h models 30h-4Fh, 60h-7Fh, 90h-91h, A0h-AFh) with
      AVX leak vector-register data after a mispredicted vzeroupper
      (CVE-2023-20593). The mitigation is the DE_CFG bit 9 chicken bit, set on
      every CPU unless the microcode revision is the fixed one for the model.
      Cost: a small FP-backup slowdown on an unfixed microcode.
      n: not looked at, the bit is left as the firmware set it.

    config X86_MIT_ZENBLEED_NEVER
        bool "n: do not look at it, do nothing"
    config X86_MIT_ZENBLEED_PROBE
        bool "probe: mitigate when the CPU is affected, say so when the tree cannot"
    config X86_MIT_ZENBLEED_REQUIRE
        bool "require: refuse to boot on an affected CPU the tree cannot mitigate"
endchoice

endif # ARCH_X86_64

if ARCH_AARCH64

comment "aarch64 speculative-execution mitigations (n / probe / require)"
comment "Chosen by probe: the ID registers first, the MIDR lists (Linux's) only for what has no bit."
comment "Each core is decided from its own MIDR and ID registers, on every CPU (boot and secondaries)."
comment "OPEN (probe prints `vulnerable`, require refuses): the per-EL0-entry ones, none is in the vector slots yet."

choice
    prompt "Spectre v2 (branch target injection): CSV2"
    default A64_MIT_SPECTRE_V2_PROBE
    help
      A core whose branch predictor lets one context steer another's
      indirect branches (CVE-2017-5715). ID_AA64PFR0_EL1.CSV2 >= 1 says it
      cannot; an in-order Cortex-A35/A53/A55/A510 is not exposed, and
      neither is a core whose firmware answers SMCCC_ARCH_WORKAROUND_1 with
      "not affected". Any other core needs the firmware call
      SMCCC_ARCH_WORKAROUND_1 on every entry from EL0 (Cortex-A57/A72/A73/
      A75): that call is NOT in the tree yet, so on such a core probe prints
      `spectre_v2=vulnerable` (the line says whether the firmware offers the
      call) and require refuses to boot, on a secondary by powering off.
      The Raspberry Pi 3's Cortex-A53 is not exposed.
      Cost when the call lands: one firmware call (SMC/HVC, some hundred
      cycles) per entry from EL0. Now: nothing, and one firmware query per
      CPU at boot on a core that needs the answer.
      n: not looked at.

    config A64_MIT_SPECTRE_V2_NEVER
        bool "n: do not look at it, do nothing"
    config A64_MIT_SPECTRE_V2_PROBE
        bool "probe: mitigate when the core is affected, say so when the tree cannot"
    config A64_MIT_SPECTRE_V2_REQUIRE
        bool "require: refuse to boot on an affected core the tree cannot mitigate"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "Spectre v4 (speculative store bypass): SSBS"
    default A64_MIT_SSB_PROBE
    help
      A load may run ahead of an older store to the same address
      (CVE-2018-3639). The in-order Cortex-A35/A53/A55/A510 are not exposed;
      a core with FEAT_SSBS (ID_AA64PFR1_EL1.SSBS >= 1) is closed by clearing
      PSTATE.SSBS on every CPU and SCTLR_EL1.DSSBS for every exception entry,
      kernel and ring 3 alike (done at boot, crates/core/arch-aarch64/src/ext/
      mitigations.rs, no cost to speak of on the cores that have it). A core
      without it whose firmware implements SMCCC_ARCH_WORKAROUND_2 gets
      the call WORKAROUND_2(1) once per CPU at boot, through the PSCI
      conduit (the Cortex-A76 of the Raspberry Pi 5 has no FEAT_SSBS):
      store bypass stays off for kernel and ring 3 until power-off, at the
      cost the firmware's mitigation has on that core (a few percent on
      store-to-load-heavy code). A core with neither is `vulnerable`: probe
      says so, require refuses to boot.
      n: not looked at; PSTATE.SSBS is left as the firmware set it and
      the firmware is not called.

    config A64_MIT_SSB_NEVER
        bool "n: do not look at it, do nothing"
    config A64_MIT_SSB_PROBE
        bool "probe: mitigate when the core is affected, say so when the tree cannot"
    config A64_MIT_SSB_REQUIRE
        bool "require: refuse to boot on an affected core the tree cannot mitigate"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "Spectre-BHB (branch history injection)"
    default A64_MIT_BHB_PROBE
    help
      Branch history from EL0 steers predictions after a return to EL1
      (CVE-2022-23960). FEAT_ECBHB (ID_AA64MMFR1_EL1.ECBHB) clears it in
      hardware; a core with FEAT_CLRBHB needs one `clrbhb` on entry from EL0,
      and the cores in Linux's loop table (Cortex-A57/A72: 8 branches;
      A76/A76AE/A77/N1: 24; A78 family, X1/X1C, A710, X2, N2, V1: 32) need
      that many taken branches, or the firmware's SMCCC_ARCH_WORKAROUND_3.
      None of these entry-time mitigations is in the vector slots yet, so on
      such a core probe prints `bhb=vulnerable` and require refuses to boot
      (the Raspberry Pi 5's Cortex-A76 needs k=24). A core on no list and
      without the bits reads as not affected: the Cortex-A53 of the
      Raspberry Pi 3. The table is Linux's as remembered, not checked
      against Arm's update; a core it has never seen reads as not affected
      unless its firmware answers SMCCC_ARCH_WORKAROUND_3 with "needed"
      (asked of every unlisted core without CLRBHB/ECBHB: one firmware
      query per CPU; a listed core is affected whatever the firmware says).
      Cost when the loop lands: k taken branches (k <= 32) per entry from
      EL0, some tens of cycles.
      n: not looked at.

    config A64_MIT_BHB_NEVER
        bool "n: do not look at it, do nothing"
    config A64_MIT_BHB_PROBE
        bool "probe: mitigate when the core is affected, say so when the tree cannot"
    config A64_MIT_BHB_REQUIRE
        bool "require: refuse to boot on an affected core the tree cannot mitigate"
endchoice

endif # ARCH_AARCH64

comment "Not implemented (no symbol until the code exists):"

comment "  branch-predictor / buffer flush on a switch between address spaces"

comment "  kernel page-table isolation (KPTI-equivalent; X86_MIT_MELTDOWN only reports it)"

comment "  speculation barrier on syscall entry"

